      ":question/worked-solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-8t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-8t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2-8t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+8}-\\sqrt{2-8a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-8a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent.",
